  "query_preview": "(Today through Thursday)...\nIssued at 308 AM EDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nPleasant yet increasingly dry pattern to continue through at least\nearly next week, if not the entire forecast period...due to several\nfactors, starting with stacked ridging over Indiana.  Western North\nAmerican trough splitting into active northern jet and southern\ncut-off will promote strengthening H500 ridging, which will extend\nfrom central Mexico to much of southeastern Canada by the start of\nthe weekend, placing the local region under its eastern half.  While\nsame, nearly-stationary surface high pressure presently occupying\nmost of the continent's eastern half...shifts to the Atlantic coast\nby Monday while still providing dry lower levels back into the\nMidwest.\n\nThis will translate to much of the same fair conditions seen so far\nthis week, with subtle moderating trend to continue as thermal ridge\naxis aloft sliding over Indiana Sunday-Monday brings unseasonable\nwarmth nearing 75F.  Remarkably persistent calm/light winds should\nalso continue to be the rule as pattern directs more active weekend\nstorm well north across interior Canada.  Likely a few clouds will\nreturn as small weaknesses embedded in the upper ridge cross the CWA.\nPatchy late night/early morning fog will also be a mainly isolated\nhazard through Sunday morning.\n\nThe latter half of the period will find the impressive ridge\nshrinking southward, allowing the next chilly air mass to slide east\nalong the US-Canada border and smaller forcing to take a northern\npath into the Upper Midwest. Expect partly to mostly cloudy skies\nfrom associated fetch of moisture from southwest...yet continued \nmild conditions amid light southerly breezes. Low confidence in \nany precipitation in central Indiana through Thursday...ahead of \nsome rain potential around the Halloween weekend.",
